@page "/toolings/source-generators"
@inherits AppComponentBase

<PageOutlet Url="toolings/source-generators"
            Title="bit Source Generators - Toolings"
            Description="bit Source Generators write the repetitive code of a Blazor app at compile time, so developers and AI assistants write, read and pay for less code." />

<div class="page-container">
    <BitText Typography="BitTypography.H3" Element="h1" Gutter>bit Source Generators</BitText>
    <br />
    <BitText Typography="BitTypography.H5" Gutter>Less code to write, read and pay for</BitText>
    <br />
    <BitText Typography="BitTypography.Subtitle1" Gutter>
        We care about how much code it takes to build an app. Every line a developer or an AI assistant writes has to be read, reviewed and kept working,
        and with AI every line is also tokens you pay for. bit Source Generators write the repetitive code while your app compiles,
        so you and your assistant only write what's specific to your app.
    </BitText>
    <CodeBox>dotnet add package Bit.SourceGenerators</CodeBox>
    Every bit Boilerplate project has it already.
    <br />
    <br />

    <section class="section-card">
        <div class="section-card-txt">
            <BitText Typography="BitTypography.H5" Gutter>Why less code matters</BitText>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Lower AI costs.</b> An assistant reads and writes fewer lines, so it uses fewer tokens, finishes sooner and has less room to get things wrong.</li>
                <li><b>Faster reviews.</b> What's left to review is the code that matters.</li>
                <li><b>Never out of date.</b> Generated code follows your declarations on every build, so a constructor or an API client can't drift from what it serves.</li>
                <li><b>Faster apps.</b> Generated code does at compile time what would otherwise take reflection at runtime.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-card">
        <div class="section-card-txt">
            <BitText Typography="BitTypography.H5" Gutter>[AutoInject]</BitText>
            Instead of a constructor that lists every service and assigns each one, mark the fields:
            <CodeBox HideCopyButton>public partial class OrderService
{
    [AutoInject] private IOrderController orderController = default!;
    [AutoInject] private ILogger&lt;OrderService> logger = default!;
}</CodeBox>
            In a class, the generator writes the constructor, and passes on whatever a base class injects as well.
            In a Blazor component, it writes the <code>[Inject]</code> property behind each field, so a component injects services the same way.
            The class has to be <code>partial</code>, and <code>BITGEN001</code> says so when it isn't.
            <br />
            <br />
            The attribute is your project's own, in the <code>Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection</code> namespace:
            <CodeBox>namespace Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;

[AttributeUsage(AttributeTargets.Field | AttributeTargets.Property, Inherited = false, AllowMultiple = false)]
public sealed class AutoInjectAttribute : Attribute
{
}</CodeBox>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-card">
        <div class="section-card-txt">
            <BitText Typography="BitTypography.H5" Gutter>Fast Blazor parameters</BitText>
            Blazor sets a component's parameters through reflection.
            For every <code>partial</code> component with <code>[Parameter]</code> or <code>[CascadingParameter]</code> properties,
            the generator writes a <code>SetParametersAsync</code> that assigns each one directly instead:
            <CodeBox HideCopyButton>public override Task SetParametersAsync(ParameterView parameters)
{
    var __hasUnknownParameters = false;
    foreach (var parameter in parameters)
    {
        switch (parameter.Name)
        {
            case nameof(Title):
               Title = (string)parameter.Value!;
               break;
            default:
               __hasUnknownParameters = true;
               break;
        }
    }

    return base.SetParametersAsync(__hasUnknownParameters ? parameters : ParameterView.Empty);
}</CodeBox>
            There's nothing to write but <code>partial</code>, and components render faster, most of all in the browser, where reflection costs the most.
            A component that declares its own <code>SetParametersAsync</code> keeps it.
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-card">
        <div class="section-card-txt">
            <BitText Typography="BitTypography.H5" Gutter>API clients, written for you</BitText>
            In a bit Boilerplate project, an API is declared once, as an interface the server and the client share:
            <CodeBox HideCopyButton>[Route("api/[controller]/[action]/")]
public interface IProductController : IAppController
{
    [HttpGet("{id}")]
    Task&lt;ProductDto> Get(Guid id, CancellationToken cancellationToken);

    [HttpPost]
    Task&lt;ProductDto> Create(ProductDto dto, CancellationToken cancellationToken);
}</CodeBox>
            The server's controller implements it, and the generator writes the client's side of every method: the URL, the query string,
            the JSON body and the response. <code>services.AddTypedHttpClients()</code> registers them all, and the client calls the API like a local service:
            <CodeBox HideCopyButton>[AutoInject] private IProductController productController = default!;

var product = await productController.Get(id, CurrentCancellationToken);</CodeBox>
            <ul>
                <li>Route and query parameters, JSON bodies and streamed <code>IAsyncEnumerable</code> responses all work.</li>
                <li>While a page prerenders, the response the server already fetched is handed to the browser, so the page doesn't ask twice.</li>
                <li>A route template that isn't valid fails the build with <code>BITGEN003</code>, rather than a request at runtime.</li>
            </ul>
            It builds on the types a bit Boilerplate project defines, so it comes with bit Boilerplate.
            Its <a href="/templates/wiki#api-odata">wiki</a> covers the rest.
        </div>
    </section>
</div>

<NavigationButtons Prev="Code Analyzers" PrevUrl="@Urls.CodeAnalyzers" />
